***(
    ltl_trace_monitor.maude

    Model-based trace checking for Linoleum, in the style of
    linoleum/docs/design.md.  It checks an LTL formula over a stream of
    spanStart / spanEnd messages (as defined in maude/linoleum/trace.maude)
    using the LTL machinery of model-checker.maude.

    ============================================================================
    Design
    ============================================================================

    The unit of observation is one message ("one message = one letter").  A
    trace is therefore a finite/infinite sequence of messages, and the LTL
    atoms are named boolean predicates over a single message.

    Instead of re-runing a whole-system model check for every prefix, the
    monitor keeps the *residual* of the formula:

        residual(eps, f)      = f                      (eps = empty trace)
        residual(w . m, f)    = progress(m, residual(w, f))

    where progress advances the formula by one message (the classic LTL
    "formula progression" / derivative):

        progress(m, True)     = True
        progress(m, False)    = False
        progress(m, ~ f)      = not(progress(m, f))
        progress(m, f /\ g)   = progress(m, f) /\ progress(m, g)
        progress(m, f \/ g)   = progress(m, f) \/ progress(m, g)
        progress(m, a)        = True   if a holds in m, else False   (a atomic)
        progress(m, O f)      = f
        progress(m, f U g)    = progress(m, g) \/ (progress(m, f) /\ (f U g))
        progress(m, f R g)    = progress(m, g) /\ (progress(m, f) \/ (f R g))

    Note there is no O around the recursive (f U g) / (f R g).  This is the
    one-step derivative D_m(f), defined by: for every trace u,

        m . u |= f   iff   u |= D_m(f)

    so the residual is exactly what the (unknown) continuation must satisfy.
    (Wrapping the recursion in O would shift the obligation by one message and
    lose the current position whenever the until is discharged there.)

    The residual r describes exactly the set of infinite continuations that
    satisfy the original formula.  Its three-valued verdict is decided with
    the LTL validity engine of model-checker.maude (SAT-SOLVER / tautCheck):

        r is valid (tautology)          => satisfied
        r is unsatisfiable (~r valid)   => violated
        otherwise                       => undecided

    This is why model checking reduces to a decision over the residual:
    tautCheck decides LTL validity over infinite words, so a valid residual is
    satisfied by *every* future, an unsatisfiable one by *no* future, and
    anything else still depends on the unobserved future.

    The state is small (a formula) and each message only advances it, so a
    potentially unbounded event stream is handled with constant memory and
    without ever re-exploring past events.

    ============================================================================
    Usage
    ============================================================================

    The expected environment is the Linoleum one: model-checker.maude and
    maude/linoleum/trace.maude are already loaded before this program (see
    maude/DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md and the lotrbot_*.maude examples).

        reduce init(isSpanEnd U isRootSpanStart) .                  *** monitor
        reduce verdict(consume(Msg1, consume(Msg2, init(F)))) .    *** Verdict
        reduce verdict(run(Msg1 ;; Msg2 ;; Msg3 ;; mt, init(F))) .  *** same
        reduce finalize(run(Msgs ;; mt, init(F))) .                 *** closed trace

    New atomic propositions are added by importing this module and declaring
    atoms plus equations for _holds_:

        op isImageGenEnd : -> Atom [ctor] .
        eq isImageGenEnd holds spanEnd(M, S) = isImageGenerationSpan(S) .

    The formula may use the whole LTL syntax of model-checker.maude: ~, /\,
    \/, O_, _U_, _R_, plus the derived ->, <>, [], W, |->, =>, <=>.
)

*** Everything is in a system module because trace.maude is an object module.
mod LTL-TRACE-MONITOR is
  *** Message/span/event data model and the LinoleumEvent messages.
  pr TRACE-CLASS-OBJECTS .
  *** LTL syntax, negative normal form, and derived temporal operators.
  pr LTL-SIMPLIFIER .
  *** tautCheck : Formula ~> TautCheckResult, backed by the native LTL solver.
  pr SAT-SOLVER .

  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***
  *** Alphabet: named boolean predicates over a single message.
  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***

  sort Atom .
  subsort Atom < Formula .

  *** Built-in atoms.  Extend with more constructors in an importing module.
  op isSpanStart : -> Atom [ctor] .
  op isSpanEnd : -> Atom [ctor] .
  op isRootSpanStart : -> Atom [ctor] .
  op isRootSpanEnd : -> Atom [ctor] .
  op startNamed : String -> Atom [ctor] .
  op endNamed : String -> Atom [ctor] .

  *** Satisfaction of an atom by a message.  The runtime hook for adding new
  *** atomic propositions is to add more equations for this operator.
  *** frozen: a message is a value, it must not be rewritten while inspected.
  op _holds_ : Atom Msg -> Bool [frozen] .

  vars M O : Oid .
  var S : Object .
  var N : String .

  eq isSpanStart holds spanStart(M, S) = true .
  eq isSpanStart holds spanEnd(M, S) = false .
  eq isSpanEnd holds spanStart(M, S) = false .
  eq isSpanEnd holds spanEnd(M, S) = true .

  eq isRootSpanStart holds spanStart(M, S) = isRootSpan(S) .
  eq isRootSpanStart holds spanEnd(M, S) = false .
  eq isRootSpanEnd holds spanEnd(M, S) = isRootSpan(S) .
  eq isRootSpanEnd holds spanStart(M, S) = false .

  eq startNamed(N) holds spanStart(M, < O : Span | name : N >) = true .
  eq startNamed(N) holds spanStart(M, S) = false [owise] .
  eq startNamed(N) holds spanEnd(M, S) = false .
  eq endNamed(N) holds spanEnd(M, < O : Span | name : N >) = true .
  eq endNamed(N) holds spanEnd(M, S) = false [owise] .
  eq endNamed(N) holds spanStart(M, S) = false .

  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***
  *** The monitor.
  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***

  sort Verdict .
  ops satisfied violated undecided : -> Verdict [ctor] .

  *** A monitor is just the current residual formula.
  sort Monitor .
  op monitor : Formula -> Monitor [ctor] .
  op formula : Monitor -> Formula .

  op init : Formula -> Monitor .
  op consume : Msg Monitor -> Monitor .
  op verdict : Monitor -> Verdict .
  op finalize : Monitor -> Verdict .

  *** Progression (derivative) of a formula by a message.
  op progress : Msg Formula -> Formula .
  op notF : Formula -> Formula .
  op atomF : Bool -> Formula .

  *** Verdict helpers around tautCheck.
  op eval : Formula -> Verdict .
  op isTaut : Formula -> Bool .
  op isTrue : TautCheckResult -> Bool .

  vars f g : Formula .
  var m : Msg .
  var a : Atom .
  vars L C : FormulaList .

  --- coercion of boolean atom values into formulas
  eq atomF(true) = True .
  eq atomF(false) = False .

  --- negation of a (necessarily boolean) residual
  eq notF(True) = False .
  eq notF(False) = True .
  eq notF(f) = ~ f [owise] .

  --- progression.  The LTL equations (incl. LTL-SIMPLIFIER) normalise the
  --- residual to negative normal form, pushing ~ down to the atoms.
  eq progress(m, True) = True .
  eq progress(m, False) = False .
  eq progress(m, ~ f) = notF(progress(m, f)) .
  eq progress(m, f /\ g) = progress(m, f) /\ progress(m, g) .
  eq progress(m, f \/ g) = progress(m, f) \/ progress(m, g) .
  eq progress(m, O f) = f .
  eq progress(m, f U g) = progress(m, g) \/ (progress(m, f) /\ (f U g)) .
  eq progress(m, f R g) = progress(m, g) /\ (progress(m, f) \/ (f R g)) .
  eq progress(m, a) = atomF(a holds m) .

  --- tautCheck returns either the Bool true, or a counterexample term.
  eq isTrue(true) = true .
  eq isTrue(counterexample(L, C)) = false .
  eq isTaut(f) = isTrue(tautCheck(f)) .

  eq eval(f) =
    if isTaut(f) then satisfied
    else if isTaut(~ f) then violated
    else undecided
    fi fi .

  --- monitor API.  Normalisation of the formula happens automatically
  --- because constructor arguments are reduced before equations are tried.
  eq init(f) = monitor(f) .
  eq consume(m, monitor(f)) = monitor(progress(m, f)) .
  eq formula(monitor(f)) = f .
  eq verdict(monitor(f)) = eval(f) .

  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***
  *** Closing a finite trace.
  ***
  *** verdict/1 above is the open-future (infinite trace) question: it can
  *** report "violated" as soon as an obligation is broken, but it reports
  *** "satisfied" only when the residual itself is valid, so a liveness under
  *** an always (e.g. [] (start -> <> end)) stays "undecided" until the trace
  *** is closed.  finalize/1 answers the finite-trace question by assuming
  *** that no further events occur (every atom is false from now on) and
  *** evaluating the residual on that constant suffix.
  ***
  *** On a constant all-false suffix, f U g and f R g both reduce to g (a
  *** state that fails once fails forever), which is the "once the window is
  *** closed, what is left of the obligation" semantics.
  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***

  op wk : Formula -> Bool .

  eq wk(True) = true .
  eq wk(False) = false .
  eq wk(~ f) = not wk(f) .
  eq wk(f /\ g) = wk(f) and wk(g) .
  eq wk(f \/ g) = wk(f) or wk(g) .
  eq wk(O f) = wk(f) .
  eq wk(f U g) = wk(g) .
  eq wk(f R g) = wk(g) .
  eq wk(a) = false .

  eq finalize(monitor(f)) = if wk(f) then satisfied else violated fi .

  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***
  *** Convenience: replay a whole list of messages.
  *** ------------------------------------------------------------------ ***

  sort MsgList .
  subsort Msg < MsgList .
  op mt : -> MsgList [ctor] .
  op _;;_ : MsgList MsgList -> MsgList [ctor assoc id: mt] .

  op run : MsgList Monitor -> Monitor .

  var Ms : Msg .
  var Rest : MsgList .
  var Mon : Monitor .
  eq run(mt, Mon) = Mon .
  eq run(Ms ;; Rest, Mon) = run(Rest, consume(Ms, Mon)) .
endm
